# --- secrets: every credential-bearing path, ignored from the first commit ---
users.json
.env
exports/
ikev2/.env

# Kept for a path a reader might create by hand, not for one any document asks
# them to. dnstt/SETUP.md § 2 still names `dnstt-server -gen-key -privkey-file
# dnstt/keys/server.key`, but under "Nothing writes a key into this checkout,
# and you should not either" -- it is what the document used to walk people
# through, kept as the thing being warned against. So grep that file and the
# rule still has something concrete to point at, which is the difference between
# a check and a claim; what it points at is a retired step, which is why this is
# belt and braces rather than cover for a documented one. Directory-wide and
# with no allowlisted exception, so a second file dropped beside the key is
# covered too. What the stack does itself never lands here: `protocol on dnstt`
# parses `-gen-key`'s stdout and stores both halves in /etc/vpn-stack/secrets.
dnstt/keys/*

# There is deliberately NO rule for a rendered credential in this tree, because
# nothing renders one here: render() writes only into
# /etc/vpn-stack/rendered-<stamp>-<rand>/, 0600 under a 0700 parent. Rules stood
# for sing-box/vless-reality/ and sing-box/hysteria2/ -- the three-sibling fragment
# layout, gone: everything renders into one directory in the state directory and
# only sing-box/common/ is tracked -- and for scripts/.deploy_key*, which nothing
# mints any more, since CI holds no secrets and has no ssh step and install.sh
# pushes this checkout rather than cloning. An ignore for a path nothing in the
# tree ever creates is an invariant a reader cannot verify from the file, and one
# of these was cited elsewhere as carrying a `!server.pub` exception it never had.
# If a credential-bearing directory shows up in the checkout again, the fix is to
# stop writing it there; ignoring it would only keep it out of review.
#
# What is left covers what an OPERATOR can put here, plus the paths a server's own
# checkout genuinely carries -- ./.env is a symlink to /etc/vpn-stack/.env, because
# docker compose reads .env from the project directory. push.sh rsyncs with
# `--filter=':- .gitignore'`, so this file also decides what a deploy sends.

# Backups and issued client bundles. `./vpn backup` writes a tar of the WHOLE
# state directory plus the IKEv2 volume — every secret, every user's password,
# and the CA private key — under one passphrase. One landed in a commit once;
# filename-exact rules would not have caught it, so these are extension-wide.
# Write backups outside the checkout (see README): the tree is rsynced to live
# servers, and this file is what decides what goes.
*.age
*.p12
*.mobileconfig
*.sswan

# --- runtime state ---
data/
state.json
rendered
rendered-*/

# --- the Flutter client ---
# The platform directories are scaffolding: `flutter create` regenerates them in
# CI, they are large, and a hand-edited Xcode project is a file nobody can
# review. .dart_tool/package_config.json carries absolute paths into whoever ran
# `pub get` last, and Generated.xcconfig carries their FLUTTER_ROOT -- neither
# belongs in a public repo.
app/android/
# app/ios/ is COMMITTED, unlike the other four. An NEPacketTunnelProvider is a
# separate app-extension target with its own bundle identifier, entitlements and
# an App Group, and none of that survives `flutter create` -- so a regenerated
# project has no tunnel in it and there is nothing to sign. app/tool/ios_project.rb
# is how it is produced, and app/ios/.gitignore keeps the generated-per-machine
# files (Generated.xcconfig, ephemeral/, Pods/) out.
app/macos/
app/windows/
app/linux/
app/build/
app/.dart_tool/
app/.flutter-plugins*
app/ephemeral/

# --- python tooling ---
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/

.vpn-host

# Local working notes; not part of what ships.
notes/
